.TH RC.CONF 4 "30 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
rc.conf \- which boot services run, and their settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/rc.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B /etc/rc.conf
turns the boot services of the add-on packages on and off and holds their
settings.
The installer creates it, the administrator edits it, and no package owns
it, so installing, updating or removing a package never changes it.
A package can therefore re-create a service's start link in
.B /etc/rc2.d
on every update without turning on a service you turned off.
.PP
Each line is one of:
.TP
.IB service = yes \fR|\fP no
whether
.I service
runs at boot.
.TP
.IB service _ key = value
one setting of
.IR service ,
named in that service's own manual page.
.PP
A line that starts with
.B #
is a comment, and so is the rest of a line from a blank followed by
.BR # .
Blank lines are ignored.
There are no blanks around the
.BR = .
A value may be put in double or single quotes, which are removed; nothing
else in it is interpreted.
When a name is given twice, the last line counts.
.PP
The file is read, never run as a script, so a line in it can do nothing but
give a value.
.SH DEFAULTS
A service that has no line here gets the default its own boot script
names; each service's manual page says which.
A value other than
.B yes
or
.B no
is treated as missing, and the boot log says so.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.ft CW
# /etc/rc.conf
syslogd=yes
display=no        # no graphical login, whatever /etc/display says
ifup_dhcpwait=30  # wait longer for a DHCP lease at boot
.ft R
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/rc.conf
this file.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rc.log
the boot log, where a bad value is reported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rc.subr (1M),
.BR display (4),
.BR ifup (1M)
.SH NOTES
The stock services in
.B /etc/rc2.d
do not read this file; only those of the add-on packages do.
